= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume $a<b$. A <Chebyshev system> of dimension $n+1$ is a family of real <continuous functions> $u_0,\ldots,u_n$ for which every <linear combination> $u=\sum_{j=0}^n c_ju_j$ with coefficients not all zero has at most $n$ distinct zeros in the interval. In particular the <functions> are <linearly independent>: an identically zero nontrivial combination would violate that bound. Multiplicities are not counted in this definition.

For distinct points $x_0,\ldots,x_n$, form the evaluation <matrix> $A=(u_j(x_i))_{i,j=0}^n$. If its <determinant> vanished, a nonzero vector $c$ would satisfy $Ac=0$. Its associated combination would vanish at all $n+1$ points, contradicting the <Chebyshev system> property. Conversely, if the property failed, some nonzero coefficient vector would give a combination vanishing at $n+1$ distinct points. At those points $Ac=0$, so the evaluation <matrix> would be singular. We have proved the <determinant criterion for a Chebyshev system>:
$$
\boxed{\Phi\text{ is a Chebyshev system}\iff
\det(u_j(x_i))_{i,j=0}^n\ne0\text{ for every set of distinct }x_i.}
$$
This is also the existence-and-uniqueness condition for interpolation from the span of the <functions>: any prescribed $n+1$ data values give a uniquely solvable linear system.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The exponents are real, as required for this family of real <continuous functions>. We prove the <exponential Chebyshev system> property by induction on the number of <functions>. A single nonzero multiple of an <exponential function> has no zero, so the base case holds.

Suppose the result holds for $n$ distinct real exponents, and consider a nontrivial combination $F(x)=\sum_{j=0}^n c_je^{\lambda_jx}$. If it had $n+1$ distinct zeros, so would
$$
g(x)=e^{-\lambda_0x}F(x)=c_0+\sum_{j=1}^n c_je^{(\lambda_j-\lambda_0)x},
$$
since the prefactor is everywhere positive. Applying <Rolle's theorem> between consecutive zeros gives at least $n$ distinct zeros of
$$
g'(x)=\sum_{j=1}^n c_j(\lambda_j-\lambda_0)e^{(\lambda_j-\lambda_0)x}.
$$
The $n$ exponents in this <derivative> are distinct. By induction, a nontrivial combination of them has at most $n-1$ zeros. Therefore every coefficient $c_j(\lambda_j-\lambda_0)$ must be zero. Distinctness gives $c_j=0$ for $j\ge1$. Then $g=c_0$ has a zero only if $c_0=0$, contradicting the original nontriviality. Consequently $F$ has at most $n$ zeros, and
$$
\boxed{(e^{\lambda_0x},\ldots,e^{\lambda_nx})\text{ is a Chebyshev system on }[a,b].}
$$

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $d$ be the dimension of a proposed real <Chebyshev system> on the circle, and regard its <functions> as continuous $2\pi$-periodic <functions> on the real line. Choose lifted points $\theta_0<\theta_1<\cdots<\theta_{d-1}<\theta_0+2\pi$ and set $\theta_d=\theta_0+2\pi$. Move the points by
$$
\theta_i(s)=(1-s)\theta_i+s\theta_{i+1},\qquad 0\le s\le1,\quad 0\le i<d.
$$
For every $s$ the points remain distinct on the circle. Indeed, each consecutive gap is a convex combination of positive gaps; the closing gap is also a convex combination of the original closing gap and $\theta_1-\theta_0$. The evaluation <determinant>
$$
D(s)=\det(u_j(\theta_i(s)))_{i,j=0}^{d-1}
$$
is therefore nonzero for every $s$ by the <determinant criterion for a Chebyshev system>. It is continuous, so the <intermediate value theorem> forbids a sign change. At $s=1$, however, periodicity makes its rows the cyclic permutation of the initial rows, giving $D(1)=(-1)^{d-1}D(0)$. For even $d$ this reverses the sign, a contradiction. Hence **every such periodic Chebyshev system has odd dimension; none has even dimension**. This <determinant> argument avoids assuming differentiability or simple zeros of the <functions>.

= 2
{parent=Paper 75}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <Korovkin theorem> states that if <positive linear operators on continuous functions> $L_n:C[0,1]\to C[0,1]$ satisfy
$$
\|L_ne_j-e_j\|_\infty\longrightarrow0,\qquad e_j(t)=t^j,\quad j=0,1,2,
$$
then $\|L_nf-f\|_\infty\to0$ for every real <continuous function> $f$.

Let $r=k-1\ge2$ and $h_n=|\Delta_n|$. For the <B-splines> in this question, $N_{i,n}\ge0$ and their sum is one on the basic interval. Thus the <Schoenberg spline operator> $V_n$ is linear and positive. To verify the three test <functions>, suppress the index $n$ and write the <Marsden identity> with $\omega_i(x)=\prod_{j=1}^r(x-t_{i+j})$. Comparing the coefficients of $x^r,x^{r-1},x^{r-2}$ gives the <monomial B-spline coefficients>
$$
1=\sum_iN_i(t),\qquad
 t=\sum_i\xi_iN_i(t),\qquad
 t^2=\sum_iq_iN_i(t),
$$
where
$$
\xi_i=\frac1r\sum_{j=1}^rt_{i+j},\qquad
q_i=\frac1{\binom r2}\sum_{1\le j<\ell\le r}t_{i+j}t_{i+\ell}.
$$
The first coefficient is the partition of unity; the second is the <Greville abscissa>. Every internal knot and the sample point $\tau_i$ lie in $[t_i,t_{i+k}]$, of width at most $kh_n$. Consequently $|\tau_i-\xi_i|\le kh_n$. Since all these numbers lie in $[0,1]$, for any two internal knots $u,v$ we also have
$$
|\tau_i^2-uv|\le|\tau_i|\,|\tau_i-u|+|u|\,|\tau_i-v|\le2kh_n.
$$
Averaging gives $|\tau_i^2-q_i|\le2kh_n$. Nonnegative weights of sum one now yield
$$
\|V_n1-1\|_\infty=0,\qquad
\|V_ne_1-e_1\|_\infty\le kh_n,\qquad
\|V_ne_2-e_2\|_\infty\le2kh_n.
$$
Here the <spline> order $k$ is fixed, so all three errors tend to zero. The <Korovkin theorem> therefore proves
$$
\boxed{\|V_nf-f\|_\infty\longrightarrow0\quad\text{for every }f\in C[0,1].}
$$
No special selection such as the <Greville abscissae> is required for the sample points. In fact the <local-support error bound for a Schoenberg spline operator> also gives $\|V_nf-f\|_\infty\le\omega(f,kh_n)$. Under the usual continuous-spline convention the operators have the codomain required by the stated theorem. If the permissive knot notation is taken to allow full interior multiplicities and hence discontinuous <splines>, the same conclusion in the uniform <norm> still follows from this direct bound, although each approximant need not then be continuous.

= 3
{parent=Paper 75}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

For a real <continuous function> on $[-1,1]$, the <Chebyshev alternation theorem> gives a unique <best uniform approximation> $p_n^*$ from the space of <polynomials> of degree at most $n$. If $f$ is not already in that space, a candidate $p$ is best if and only if there exist ordered points $x_0<\cdots<x_{n+1}$ and a sign $\sigma\in\{1,-1\}$ with
$$
 f(x_j)-p(x_j)=\sigma(-1)^j\|f-p\|_\infty,
 \qquad 0\le j\le n+1.
$$
Thus a nonzero best error has **at least $n+2$ alternating extrema of equal magnitude**. If $f$ is already a <polynomial> of degree at most $n$, the unique best approximant is $f$ itself with zero error.

Existence does not require assuming that the infimum is attained: a minimizing sequence has uniformly bounded <polynomial> <norms>, and evaluation at $n+1$ fixed distinct nodes bounds its coefficients through an invertible interpolation <matrix>. A convergent coefficient subsequence then attains the infimum. The alternation criterion characterizes that minimum and gives uniqueness. In the following parts, $n\ge1$ whenever $E_{n-1}$ occurs.

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A nontrivial example is $f_n=T_{n+1}$, the <Chebyshev polynomial> characterized by $T_{n+1}(\cos\theta)=\cos((n+1)\theta)$. Its <supremum norm> is one. At the $n+2$ points $\cos(j\pi/(n+1))$, $0\le j\le n+1$, its values alternate between $1$ and $-1$; reversing their order gives increasing nodes with the same alternation.

The zero <polynomial> therefore satisfies the <Chebyshev alternation theorem> for degree at most $n$, so $E_n(f_n)=1$. Since the smaller <polynomial> space is contained in the larger one, $E_{n-1}(f_n)\ge1$; the zero <polynomial> is feasible in the smaller space as well, giving the reverse inequality. Hence
$$
\boxed{f_n=T_{n+1},\qquad E_{n-1}(f_n)=E_n(f_n)=1.}
$$
A constant <function> would also give equality with both errors zero, but this example shows that equality can occur at a positive approximation error.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $p$ be the <best uniform approximation> to the <exponential function> from the degree-at-most-$n-1$ <polynomial> space. Suppose that $E_{n-1}(e^x)=E_n(e^x)$. Then the same $p$ is best in the larger space. Its error $g(x)=e^x-p(x)$ is nonzero, since the exponential is not a <polynomial>. The <Chebyshev alternation theorem> supplies $n+2$ ordered points where $g$ has alternating signs. By the <intermediate value theorem>, $g$ has at least one zero in each of the $n+1$ intervening intervals, hence at least $n+1$ distinct zeros.

Applying <Rolle's theorem> successively $n$ times forces a zero of $g^{(n)}$. But $\deg p\le n-1$, so
$$
g^{(n)}(x)=e^x>0
$$
everywhere. This is a contradiction. Together with the automatic non-increase of best errors, it proves
$$
\boxed{E_{n-1}(e^x)>E_n(e^x),\qquad n\ge1.}
$$
The argument is an instance of <strict polynomial error decrease from nonvanishing derivatives>.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For each $n$, choose the <best uniform approximation> $p_n^*$. If its error is nonzero, take its $n+2$ alternating extrema. The <intermediate value theorem> supplies a point $y_j$ strictly between the $j$th and $(j+1)$st extrema such that $f(y_j)=p_n^*(y_j)$, for $0\le j\le n$. These $n+1$ points are distinct. The <polynomial interpolation> problem at them has a unique solution of degree at most $n$, so its solution is exactly $p_n^*$. This constructs the <interpolation nodes from best uniform approximation>.

If $E_n(f)=0$, then $f$ itself belongs to the <polynomial> space, and any $n+1$ distinct nodes give the same conclusion. In either case, take $x_{n,j}=y_j$ or the arbitrary nodes just described. Then
$$
\|\ell_n(f)-f\|_\infty=E_n(f).
$$
The <Weierstrass approximation theorem> gives $E_n(f)\to0$; alternatively, the bound derived in part 4(b), combined with <uniform continuity>, gives this convergence directly. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\|\ell_n(f)-f\|_\infty\longrightarrow0.}
$$
The nodes may depend on $f$ and on $n$. The conclusion does not assert a universal sequence of interpolation grids working for every continuous target.

= 4
{parent=Paper 75}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <function> on an interval, the <modulus of continuity> is $\omega(f,t)=\sup_{|x-y|\le t}|f(x)-f(y)|$, with the pair restricted to that interval. For a periodic <function> the equivalent definition is $\sup_{\theta,|h|\le t}|g(\theta+h)-g(\theta)|$. The <mean value theorem> and $|\sin\theta|\le1$ give
$$
|\cos(\theta+h)-\cos\theta|\le|h|.
$$
Both cosine values lie in $[-1,1]$. Hence for $|h|\le t$,
$$
|\widetilde f(\theta+h)-\widetilde f(\theta)|
=|f(\cos(\theta+h))-f(\cos\theta)|\le\omega(f,t).
$$
Taking the supremum proves
$$
\boxed{\omega(\widetilde f,t)\le\omega(f,t).}
$$
If the periodic <modulus of continuity> is defined using circular distance instead, choose lifts at that shortest distance and apply the same argument. The <cosine substitution for polynomial approximation> therefore does not enlarge this modulus.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <first Jackson theorem for periodic approximation> states that an absolute constant $C_J$ exists such that, for every continuous $2\pi$-periodic <function> $g$ and integer $n\ge1$, there is a <trigonometric polynomial> $q_n$ of degree at most $n$ satisfying
$$
\|g-q_n\|_\infty\le C_J\omega(g,1/n).
$$
Apply it to the continuous periodic <function> $g(\theta)=f(\cos\theta)$, which is even. Replace the approximant by its even part
$$
q_n^{\rm ev}(\theta)=\frac12\bigl(q_n(\theta)+q_n(-\theta)\bigr).
$$
Since $g(-\theta)=g(\theta)$, the <triangle inequality> gives $\|g-q_n^{\rm ev}\|_\infty\le\|g-q_n\|_\infty$. This symmetrization cancels all sine terms, so $q_n^{\rm ev}=\sum_{j=0}^n\alpha_j\cos(j\theta)$. The <Chebyshev polynomials> satisfy $T_j(\cos\theta)=\cos(j\theta)$ and have degree $j$; the latter follows inductively from $T_0=1$, $T_1=x$ and $T_{j+1}=2xT_j-T_{j-1}$. Therefore
$$
p_n(x)=\sum_{j=0}^n\alpha_jT_j(x)
$$
is an algebraic <polynomial> of degree at most $n$, and $p_n(\cos\theta)=q_n^{\rm ev}(\theta)$. Because cosine maps onto $[-1,1]$, the <supremum norms> agree exactly:
$$
\|f-p_n\|_{[-1,1]}=\|g-q_n^{\rm ev}\|_{\mathbb T}.
$$
Finally, the definition of the best error, periodic Jackson approximation, and part (a) give the successive bounds
$$
E_n(f)\le\|f-p_n\|_\infty
\le C_J\omega(g,1/n)
\le C_J\omega(f,1/n).
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{E_n(f)\le C_J\omega(f,1/n),\qquad n\ge1.}
$$
Neither the constant nor the choice of approximation space depends on $f$. The <cosine substitution for polynomial approximation> and even symmetrization are what preserve the required degree bound.

= 5
{parent=Paper 75}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Set $r=k-1$, with $k\ge2$ for the final linear-reproduction assertion, and use the normalized knot <polynomial> $\psi_i=\omega_i/r!$. Differentiate the <Marsden identity> $r-j$ times with respect to the auxiliary variable $x$, for $0\le j\le r$. This gives
$$
\frac{r!}{j!}(x-t)^j=\sum_i\omega_i^{(r-j)}(x)N_i(t),
\qquad
\frac{(x-t)^j}{j!}=\sum_i\psi_i^{(r-j)}(x)N_i(t).
$$
The <Taylor polynomial> expansion of an arbitrary <polynomial> $p$ of degree at most $r$ about $x$ is exact:
$$
p(t)=\sum_{j=0}^r\frac{p^{(j)}(x)}{j!}(t-x)^j.
$$
Substitute the differentiated identity into each term and collect the <B-spline> coefficients. The result is
$$
p(t)=\sum_i\lambda_i(p)N_i(t),\qquad
\boxed{\lambda_i(p)=\sum_{j=0}^r(-1)^jp^{(j)}(x)\psi_i^{(r-j)}(x).}
$$
This is the <normalized Marsden dual functional>. There is no extra factorial outside the sum, because it is already included in $\psi_i$.

To prove independence of $x$ directly, differentiate the finite sum. The contribution from differentiating $\psi_i$ in term $j$ cancels the contribution from differentiating $p$ in term $j-1$. Explicitly,
$$
\frac{d}{dx}\lambda_i(p)
=\sum_{j=0}^r(-1)^jp^{(j+1)}\psi_i^{(r-j)}
+\sum_{j=0}^r(-1)^jp^{(j)}\psi_i^{(r-j+1)}=0.
$$
The two uncancelled endpoint terms vanish because $p^{(r+1)}=\psi_i^{(r+1)}=0$. Thus the coefficient functional is constant in the auxiliary variable, as required.

The leading two terms of the knot <polynomial> give
$$
\psi_i^{(r)}(x)=1,\qquad
\psi_i^{(r-1)}(x)=x-\frac1r\sum_{j=1}^rt_{i+j}=x-t_i^*.
$$
For a linear <polynomial> $p(t)=A+Bt$, only $j=0,1$ survive, whence
$$
\lambda_i(p)=p(x)-(x-t_i^*)p'(x)=A+Bt_i^*=p(t_i^*).
$$
The coefficient points $t_i^*$ are the <Greville abscissae>. Inserting these coefficients proves exact linear reproduction:
$$
\boxed{p(t)=\sum_i p(t_i^*)N_i(t)\quad\text{for every }p\in\mathcal P_1.}
$$
All identities hold on the basic knot interval $[t_k,t_{n+1}]$, including the endpoints with the standard continuous endpoint convention. Repeated knots cause no difficulty in these <polynomial> differentiations.

= 6
{parent=Paper 75}
{scope}

= 1
{parent=6}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $r=x-u^*$. If $u^*$ is a best approximation, then for every $v\in\mathcal U_n$ and real $t$, the competitor $u^*+tv$ satisfies
$$
\|r-tv\|^2-\|r\|^2=-2t\operatorname{Re}(r,v)+t^2\|v\|^2\ge0.
$$
The linear coefficient must be zero, since otherwise choosing sufficiently small $t$ of the appropriate sign makes this expression negative. Hence $\operatorname{Re}(r,v)=0$. In a real <inner product space> this is the desired orthogonality; in a complex <inner product space>, apply the same argument to $iv$ to show that the imaginary part also vanishes. Thus $(r,v)=0$ for all $v\in\mathcal U_n$.

Conversely, suppose this orthogonality holds. Every competitor $u\in\mathcal U_n$ has $u-u^*\in\mathcal U_n$. The <Pythagorean identity> then gives
$$
\|x-u\|^2=\|r-(u-u^*)\|^2=\|r\|^2+\|u-u^*\|^2\ge\|r\|^2.
$$
Equality forces $u=u^*$. We have proved the characterization, as well as uniqueness:
$$
\boxed{u^*\text{ is best}\iff (x-u^*,v)=0\text{ for every }v\in\mathcal U_n.}
$$
This is the <orthogonal projection onto a finite-dimensional subspace>. Completeness of the ambient space is not needed. Existence follows, for example, by solving the invertible <Gram matrix> system in a <basis> of the finite-dimensional <linear subspace>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The dual vectors are the <Riesz dual basis in an inner product space>, and lie in $\mathcal U_n$ itself. Take the convention that the <inner product> is conjugate-linear in its first argument; in a real space the conjugations can simply be omitted. Since the $u_j$ form a <basis>, their <Gram matrix> $G$ is positive definite and invertible. Let $B=G^{-1}$ and put
$$
\widehat u_k=\sum_{\ell=1}^n b_{\ell k}u_\ell.
$$
The proposed vectors satisfy
$$
(u_i,\widehat u_k)=\sum_\ell G_{i\ell}b_{\ell k}=(GB)_{ik}=\delta_{ik}.
$$
They therefore give the unique dual family within $\mathcal U_n$. Their own <Gram matrix> is
$$
\bigl((\widehat u_j,\widehat u_k)\bigr)_{j,k}=B^*GB.
$$
The inverse of a Hermitian positive-definite <matrix> is Hermitian, so $B^*=B$. Using $GB=I$ gives $B^*GB=B$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{(\widehat u_j,\widehat u_k)=b_{jk}.}
$$
For a real space this is the familiar identity $B^TGB=B$. If the opposite complex inner-product convention is adopted, conjugate the coefficients in the dual-vector formula; the stated identity remains the same. Requiring the dual vectors to lie in $\mathcal U_n$ is essential: arbitrary additions perpendicular to $\mathcal U_n$ preserve the pairings with $u_i$ but alter the Gram <matrix> of the dual vectors.

= 2
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Use the standard nonnegative partition-normalized <B-splines> $N_j$; their sum is at most one, and is exactly one for the complete clamped <basis> on $[0,1]$. The printed question introduces $M_i$ only in its last <matrix> formula without defining it. The intended normalization is the <unit-integral normalization of a B-spline>:
$$
c_i=\int_0^1N_i(t)\,dt>0,\qquad M_i(t)=\frac{N_i(t)}{c_i},\qquad \int_0^1M_i(t)\,dt=1.
$$
For supports wholly inside the interval, $c_i=(t_{i+k}-t_i)/k$. The positivity and partition convention, sometimes called $L_\infty$ normalization, should not be confused with individually rescaling every <spline> to have maximum exactly one.

The characterization in part 1(a) gives the normal equations for the <orthogonal projection>:
$$
\left(f-\sum_ja_jN_j,N_i\right)_{L_2}=0,\qquad 1\le i\le n.
$$
Dividing each equation by $c_i$ yields
$$
Ga=d,\qquad G_{ij}=(M_i,N_j)_{L_2},\qquad d_i=(M_i,f)_{L_2}.
$$
This is the <mixed-normalization spline Gram matrix>. It need not be symmetric, but $G=D^{-1}H$ with $D=\operatorname{diag}(c_i)$ and $H_{ij}=(N_i,N_j)_{L_2}$. The ordinary <Gram matrix> $H$ is positive definite because the <splines> are linearly independent, so $G$ is invertible and $a=G^{-1}d$.

Since each $M_i$ is nonnegative and has integral one,
$$
|d_i|\le\int_0^1M_i(t)|f(t)|dt\le\|f\|_\infty,
\qquad\|d\|_{\ell_\infty}\le\|f\|_\infty.
$$
The induced <matrix> <operator norm> is the maximum absolute row sum, so
$$
\|a\|_{\ell_\infty}\le\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell_\infty\to\ell_\infty}\|f\|_\infty.
$$
Finally, nonnegativity and the <subpartition of unity for B-splines> give
$$
|P_{\mathcal S}f(t)|
=\left|\sum_ja_jN_j(t)\right|
\le\|a\|_{\ell_\infty}\sum_jN_j(t)
\le\|a\|_{\ell_\infty}.
$$
Taking the supremum over $t$ and then over $\|f\|_\infty\le1$ proves the <maximum-norm bound for spline projection>:
$$
\boxed{\|P_{\mathcal S}\|_\infty\le\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell_\infty\to\ell_\infty}.}
$$
The formulas also establish linearity and boundedness of the projection on continuous inputs. Its range is exactly the finite-dimensional <spline> space $\mathcal S$, since it fixes every <spline> in that space. Thus, when the <splines> are continuous, it is an operator $C[0,1]\to C[0,1]$ with range $\mathcal S$; it is not onto all of $C[0,1]$ as the printed wording says. For example, $e^t$ cannot belong to a fixed finite-degree <spline> space because it is not a <polynomial> on any nonempty knot interval. The missing $M_i$ normalization and this range correction are needed for a precise reading of the result.
